{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 43, :comments ["<b>In all swarming things that swarm.</b> It did not say 'upon the earth' as in the earlier verses, for had it said so, it would be implied that one does not make his soul an abomination except in the swarming thing of the land specifically. And it is a known thing that there are two kinds of mouse — one on land and one in the sea — and so with the rest of the swarming things that we do not know. Therefore it says: בכל השרץ השורץ — 'in all swarming things that swarm' — that is, even in the swarming thing of the sea, for whoever eats it makes his soul an abomination and defiles himself. Therefore He warned not to make his soul an abomination and not to defile himself with them." "<b>And you shall not defile yourselves with them, and you shall be defiled by them.</b> It means: ולא תטמאו בהם — 'you shall not defile yourselves with them' — through eating them, in the body; for if you do so, ונטמתם בם — 'you shall be defiled by them' — in the soul. And the word is spelled without an אל\"ף, which is an expression of blockage (טמטום), and the reason is that the heart becomes blocked through eating forbidden things, and the spirit of holiness does not rest upon it." "<b>And it is possible</b> to say that since the letter אל\"ף alludes to unity and precedence, therefore the אל\"ף is missing from the word — because the Shekhinah withdraws from a place of impurity and sin, in the manner of what is written (Deuteronomy 23:15): ״כי ה' אלהיך מתהלך בקרב מחנך״ — 'for HaShem your Elohim walks in the midst of your camp' — ״ולא יראה בך ערות דבר ושב מאחריך״ — 'and He shall see no unseemly thing in you and turn away from you.' And this is the missing of the אל\"ף in the word מחטו לי. And this same reason we may say concerning what is called קדוש in our language — one who is unified and distinguished in the service of HaShem; whereas one devoted to the act of harlotry is called קדש with the ו\"ו missing, for the ו\"ו is one of the letters of the holy Name, and therefore the ו\"ו was removed from his name, for there is no holiness in a place of impurity. And from this you can understand the power of our holy language: with a slight change of language from one word to another, mountains upon mountains depend." "And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted <b>ולא תטמאו בהם ונטמתם בם</b>: if a person defiles himself a little, they defile him much; if a person defiles himself below, they defile him above; if a person defiles himself in this world, they defile him in the World to Come. Therefore Scripture juxtaposes <b>והתקדשתם והייתם קדושים כי קדוש אני</b>: <b>והתקדשתם</b> — in the body; <b>והייתם קדושים</b> — in the soul, for the World to Come; <b>כי קדוש אני</b>."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:25:03.482875776Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}